1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,370
In 2025, five tech companies spent about

2
00:00:04,370 --> 00:00:07,691
four hundred billion dollars building AI.

3
00:00:07,991 --> 00:00:10,329
This year, they're on track to spend around

4
00:00:10,329 --> 00:00:13,363
eight hundred billion. Double, in one year.

5
00:00:13,663 --> 00:00:17,994
And the question everyone types into YouTube is: is this a bubble?

6
00:00:18,544 --> 00:00:22,483
I think that's the wrong question. The better one is: where is

7
00:00:22,483 --> 00:00:26,289
the money actually going, and what's left standing if it stops?

8
00:00:26,589 --> 00:00:30,444
So let's follow one dollar all the way around. Because

9
00:00:30,444 --> 00:00:34,013
it doesn't go in a straight line. It goes in a circle.

10
00:00:34,863 --> 00:00:37,841
Start with Nvidia. It makes the chips

11
00:00:37,841 --> 00:00:40,207
that almost every AI model runs on.

12
00:00:40,507 --> 00:00:44,341
In September 2025, Nvidia announced it intended

13
00:00:44,341 --> 00:00:48,803
to invest up to one hundred billion dollars in OpenAI.

14
00:00:49,103 --> 00:00:52,978
Read the fine print: the money would arrive in stages,

15
00:00:52,978 --> 00:00:56,579
as each gigawatt of computing power was deployed.

16
00:00:56,879 --> 00:01:00,432
Computing power built on what? Nvidia systems.

17
00:01:00,732 --> 00:01:05,385
Two weeks later, OpenAI signed a deal with Nvidia's rival,

18
00:01:05,385 --> 00:01:09,592
AMD. OpenAI would buy six gigawatts of AMD chips.

19
00:01:09,892 --> 00:01:15,355
And AMD handed OpenAI the right to buy up to 160 million AMD

20
00:01:15,355 --> 00:01:20,610
shares, unlocked step by step as OpenAI's purchases grow.

21
00:01:21,060 --> 00:01:24,409
The hundred-billion plan never got finalized.

22
00:01:24,709 --> 00:01:29,758
But this April, OpenAI closed a funding round of 122 billion

23
00:01:29,758 --> 00:01:34,573
dollars. One of the biggest checks, 30 billion, came from Nvidia.

24
00:01:35,023 --> 00:01:37,316
So: the company selling the chips puts

25
00:01:37,316 --> 00:01:39,818
money into the company buying the chips.

26
00:01:40,118 --> 00:01:43,130
The buyer uses that money to buy more chips.

27
00:01:43,430 --> 00:01:45,413
And the seller books that as revenue.

28
00:01:46,263 --> 00:01:52,703
Is that illegal? No. Is it new? Also no. It has a name: vendor financing.

29
00:01:53,253 --> 00:01:56,971
And the last time it happened at this scale, it didn't end well.

30
00:01:57,271 --> 00:02:02,037
Late 1990s. The internet boom. Lucent was one of

31
00:02:02,037 --> 00:02:05,166
the biggest telecom equipment makers in the world.

32
00:02:05,466 --> 00:02:08,718
Its customers were new internet companies racing to

33
00:02:08,718 --> 00:02:12,245
build networks, and many of them didn't have the cash.

34
00:02:12,545 --> 00:02:14,356
So Lucent lent it to them.

35
00:02:14,656 --> 00:02:18,276
In one deal it agreed to finance more than one hundred

36
00:02:18,276 --> 00:02:21,651
percent of a small carrier's equipment purchases.

37
00:02:21,951 --> 00:02:25,283
By the time the bubble burst, Lucent had about seven

38
00:02:25,283 --> 00:02:29,016
billion dollars in loan commitments to its own customers.

39
00:02:29,316 --> 00:02:32,561
Then the customers started going bankrupt. That

40
00:02:32,561 --> 00:02:36,719
small carrier filed in 2001. The loans went bad.

41
00:02:37,019 --> 00:02:39,343
Lucent's stock fell below one dollar.

42
00:02:39,793 --> 00:02:44,968
Now, Nvidia is not Lucent. Nvidia is enormously profitable.

43
00:02:45,268 --> 00:02:49,114
Last quarter alone, its data center business brought

44
00:02:49,114 --> 00:02:53,251
in 89 billion dollars, and it holds more than 50 billion

45
00:02:53,251 --> 00:02:57,262
dollars in cash and bonds. It can afford to be wrong.

46
00:02:57,562 --> 00:03:01,727
The real question isn't whether Nvidia survives. It's

47
00:03:01,727 --> 00:03:04,591
whether its customers can pay for what they've ordered.

48
00:03:04,891 --> 00:03:06,897
And for that, we need to look at who's

49
00:03:06,897 --> 00:03:08,950
paying for the eight hundred billion.

50
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:11,941
Most of it still comes from cash these

51
00:03:11,941 --> 00:03:14,976
companies earn. But not all of it anymore.

52
00:03:15,276 --> 00:03:19,044
By the end of June this year, the biggest cloud

53
00:03:19,044 --> 00:03:22,655
companies had already sold around 170 billion

54
00:03:22,655 --> 00:03:26,458
dollars of bonds in 2026. That's borrowed money.

55
00:03:26,758 --> 00:03:29,675
And some of the borrowing doesn't show up where you'd look.

56
00:03:29,975 --> 00:03:34,416
Take Meta's giant data center in Louisiana, called Hyperion.

57
00:03:34,716 --> 00:03:39,186
Meta didn't put the 27 billion dollars of debt on its own books.

58
00:03:39,486 --> 00:03:42,616
It set up a separate company: an investment

59
00:03:42,616 --> 00:03:46,038
firm, Blue Owl, owns 80 percent, Meta owns 20.

60
00:03:46,338 --> 00:03:48,219
That company borrowed the money

61
00:03:48,219 --> 00:03:51,331
and built the data center. Meta rents it.

62
00:03:51,631 --> 00:03:55,229
But here's the catch. If Meta walks away, Meta has

63
00:03:55,229 --> 00:03:59,436
promised to cover the building's value for the first 16 years.

64
00:03:59,736 --> 00:04:04,289
So the risk is Meta's. The debt just isn't on Meta's balance sheet.

65
00:04:04,589 --> 00:04:07,120
Or take Oracle, one of the companies

66
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:09,937
building computing capacity for OpenAI.

67
00:04:10,237 --> 00:04:14,387
This year alone it has sold 25 billion dollars of bonds,

68
00:04:14,387 --> 00:04:18,391
issued 5 billion of a special kind of preferred stock,

69
00:04:18,391 --> 00:04:22,035
and planned up to 20 billion more in new shares.

70
00:04:22,385 --> 00:04:25,231
That's three different ways of raising money in one

71
00:04:25,231 --> 00:04:28,584
year. When a company is reaching for every tool

72
00:04:28,584 --> 00:04:31,853
at once, it tells you how much cash this race eats.

73
00:04:32,703 --> 00:04:35,473
All that borrowing only makes sense if the

74
00:04:35,473 --> 00:04:38,788
money comes back as revenue. So does it?

75
00:04:39,088 --> 00:04:42,558
In 2024, an investor at Sequoia Capital,

76
00:04:42,558 --> 00:04:45,840
David Cahn, did a simple calculation.

77
00:04:46,140 --> 00:04:50,025
Take what companies are spending on AI chips, double it for the

78
00:04:50,025 --> 00:04:54,667
buildings and electricity, double it again so the customers make a profit.

79
00:04:54,967 --> 00:04:59,743
His answer: AI needed to earn about 600 billion dollars a year

80
00:04:59,743 --> 00:05:04,127
to justify the spending. It was earning a small fraction of that.

81
00:05:04,427 --> 00:05:08,642
Look at OpenAI, the most-used AI company in the world.

82
00:05:08,942 --> 00:05:13,733
In 2025, it made about 13 billion dollars in revenue.

83
00:05:14,033 --> 00:05:17,904
Its CEO has talked about computing commitments

84
00:05:17,904 --> 00:05:21,925
of about 1.4 trillion dollars over eight years.

85
00:05:22,225 --> 00:05:26,944
It later scaled its plan to about 600 billion through 2030,

86
00:05:26,944 --> 00:05:31,498
and projects revenue of more than 280 billion that year.

87
00:05:31,948 --> 00:05:35,326
So: from 13 billion to 280 billion in

88
00:05:35,326 --> 00:05:38,494
five years. That's about twenty times.

89
00:05:38,944 --> 00:05:42,493
Possible? Maybe. But the whole loop depends on it.

90
00:05:43,343 --> 00:05:46,173
Here's the part most bubble videos skip.

91
00:05:46,173 --> 00:05:49,835
Bubbles don't only destroy. Sometimes they build.

92
00:05:50,135 --> 00:05:55,398
Back to 2001. In the two years before the crash, telecom

93
00:05:55,398 --> 00:06:00,063
companies laid about 100 million miles of fiber optic cable.

94
00:06:00,363 --> 00:06:03,558
When the bubble burst, about 95 percent of it

95
00:06:03,558 --> 00:06:07,614
was sitting dark, unused. Investors lost fortunes.

96
00:06:07,914 --> 00:06:11,717
But the fiber stayed in the ground. Later, other companies

97
00:06:11,717 --> 00:06:15,423
bought that unused capacity cheaply and switched it on.

98
00:06:15,723 --> 00:06:18,774
The bubble was a disaster for the people who paid

99
00:06:18,774 --> 00:06:21,975
for it, and a bargain for everyone who came after.

100
00:06:22,275 --> 00:06:24,960
So will the AI bubble leave something behind

101
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:27,783
too? This is where it gets uncomfortable.

102
00:06:28,083 --> 00:06:31,997
Fiber lasts for decades. AI chips don't. A

103
00:06:31,997 --> 00:06:34,891
new generation comes out every year or two.

104
00:06:35,191 --> 00:06:37,677
Most of these companies spread the cost

105
00:06:37,677 --> 00:06:40,313
of their servers over five or six years.

106
00:06:40,613 --> 00:06:44,114
The investor Michael Burry, the one from The Big Short,

107
00:06:44,114 --> 00:06:48,202
argues that's too long for chips that go out of date this fast.

108
00:06:48,502 --> 00:06:53,372
By his estimate, it makes the industry's profits look about

109
00:06:53,372 --> 00:06:57,987
176 billion dollars bigger than they are, through 2028.

110
00:06:58,437 --> 00:07:02,129
You don't have to agree with him. But it changes the question.

111
00:07:02,979 --> 00:07:04,742
There are really two bubbles here.

112
00:07:05,042 --> 00:07:08,582
One is in prices: stocks, valuations, deals that

113
00:07:08,582 --> 00:07:12,459
loop back on themselves. That one can pop fast.

114
00:07:12,759 --> 00:07:18,064
The other is in things: buildings, power lines, grid connections, chips.

115
00:07:18,064 --> 00:07:22,660
Some of those will last decades. Some will be obsolete in three years.

116
00:07:23,010 --> 00:07:25,163
Think about what's actually hard to get

117
00:07:25,163 --> 00:07:28,481
right now. It isn't chips. It's electricity.

118
00:07:28,781 --> 00:07:33,022
Microsoft's CEO has said the company has AI chips sitting in

119
00:07:33,022 --> 00:07:37,343
inventory because it doesn't have the power to plug them in.

120
00:07:37,643 --> 00:07:42,043
And in the US, new power projects that came online in 2025

121
00:07:42,043 --> 00:07:46,966
typically waited more than five years for their grid connection.

122
00:07:47,266 --> 00:07:50,131
A building with a power connection is still worth

123
00:07:50,131 --> 00:07:52,916
something after the chips inside it are scrap.

124
00:07:53,266 --> 00:07:56,619
That may be the fiber of this bubble: not the silicon,

125
00:07:56,619 --> 00:07:59,330
but the concrete and the copper around it.

126
00:07:59,780 --> 00:08:03,706
So instead of asking "is AI a bubble," ask: which parts last?

127
00:08:04,006 --> 00:08:06,083
Because if you own an index fund,

128
00:08:06,083 --> 00:08:08,493
you already own a piece of this loop.

129
00:08:08,793 --> 00:08:13,246
My guess: the buildings and the power lines survive. The chips

130
00:08:13,246 --> 00:08:17,367
don't. What do you think survives? Tell me in the comments.

131
00:08:17,667 --> 00:08:21,815
This isn't investment advice. It's just following the money.

